@@ -107,6 +107,81 @@ Please refer to SQLAlchemy `Dialect`_ documentation for connection information i
 
 
 
+Primary Keys and Foreign Keys
+=============================
+
+Besides columns, a MetaDataProvider can also provide primary key and foreign keys of a table. These keys are shown
+alongside the table lineage. They are only informational and don't change the lineage result itself.
+
+:meth:`sqllineage.core.metadata_provider.MetaDataProvider.get_table_keys` returns a
+:class:`sqllineage.core.models.TableKeys` for a table. Column names are normalized the same way as
+``get_table_columns`` does, and foreign keys are sorted by (columns, referred_table). Unknown tables get empty keys,
+and this method never raises.
+
+.. autoclass:: sqllineage.core.models.TableKeys
+    :members: primary_key, foreign_keys
+
+.. autoclass:: sqllineage.core.models.ForeignKey
+    :members: columns, referred_table, referred_columns
+
+``SQLAlchemyMetaDataProvider`` reads the keys by database reflection. When the table doesn't exist, no keys are returned
+and a warning is logged. ``DummyMetaDataProvider`` takes the keys as two extra dicts, keyed by ``schema.table`` just
+like ``metadata``:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    >>> from sqllineage.core.metadata.dummy import DummyMetaDataProvider
+    >>> from sqllineage.core.models import ForeignKey, Table
+    >>> from sqllineage.runner import LineageRunner
+    >>> sql = """insert into db1.report
+    ... select o.order_id, c.name
+    ... from db2.orders o join db2.customers c on o.customer_id = c.id"""
+    >>> provider = DummyMetaDataProvider(
+    ...     primary_keys={"db2.customers": ["id"], "db2.orders": ["order_id"]},
+    ...     foreign_keys={
+    ...         "db2.orders": [ForeignKey(("customer_id",), Table("db2.customers"), ("id",))]
+    ...     },
+    ... )
+    >>> runner = LineageRunner(sql, metadata_provider=provider)
+    >>> runner.print_table_lineage()
+    Statements(#): 1
+    Source Tables:
+        db2.customers [PK: id]
+        db2.orders [PK: order_id; FK: customer_id -> db2.customers(id)]
+    Target Tables:
+        db1.report
+
+    >>> runner.get_table_keys()[Table("db2.orders")]
+    TableKeys(primary_key=('order_id',), foreign_keys=(ForeignKey(columns=('customer_id',), referred_table=Table: db2.customers, referred_columns=('id',)),))
+
+The command line table-level output (``sqllineage -e ... --sqlalchemy_url=...``) shows the keys the same way. In
+``LineageRunner.to_cytoscape()``, table nodes with keys get ``primary_key`` and ``foreign_keys`` attributes. At column
+level, the columns that are part of a key also get a ``keys`` attribute, like ``["PK", "FK"]``.
+
+``LineageRunner`` looks up each table at most once, and never queries a provider that has no metadata (a provider
+whose bool value is ``False``, like the default empty ``DummyMetaDataProvider``). When no keys are known, the output
+is exactly the same as before.
+
+To provide keys from your own MetaDataProvider, override the two optional hooks below in addition to
+``_get_table_columns``. Both default to returning an empty list, so existing custom providers keep working unchanged.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from sqllineage.core.metadata_provider import MetaDataProvider
+    from sqllineage.core.models import ForeignKey, Table
+
+
+    class MyMetaDataProvider(MetaDataProvider):
+        def _get_table_columns(self, schema: str, table: str, **kwargs) -> list[str]:
+            ...
+
+        def _get_primary_key(self, schema: str, table: str) -> list[str]:
+            return ["order_id"]
+
+        def _get_foreign_keys(self, schema: str, table: str) -> list[ForeignKey]:
+            return [ForeignKey(("customer_id",), Table("db2.customers"), ("id",))]
+
+
 .. _Dialect: https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/20/dialects/
 .. _snowflake-sqlalchemy: https://github.com/snowflakedb/snowflake-sqlalchemy
 .. _sqlalchemy-bigquery: https://github.com/googleapis/python-bigquery-sqlalchemy
